            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Hypatia</strong><br>\nHypatia was born around 360 AD in Alexandria, Egypt. Her father was Theon, a well-known mathematician and philosopher who instilled a love of learning in her from an early age. She was educated in Athens and Italy, which was rare for women at the time. Hypatia became renowned for her knowledge in many fields, including arts, literature, science, and philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hypatia's Work in Mathematics</strong><br>\nHypatia made significant contributions to mathematics, particularly in the field of geometry. She wrote commentaries on Diophantus's \"Arithmetica,\" Apollonius's \"Conics,\" and Ptolemy's works, although none of these texts survive to this day. She was known for her work in algebra and her efforts to teach and simplify complex mathematical concepts for her students and the public.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy and Teaching</strong><br>\nHypatia was also a dedicated Platonist philosopher. She followed the Neoplatonist school, which attempted to synthesize Plato's philosophies with other traditions. As a teacher, she attracted students from across the Mediterranean who came to learn about philosophy, astronomy, and mathematics. Her public lectures were popular because she was able to explain complex ideas in an understandable way.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
