            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Astronomy</strong><br>\nIn astronomy, Hypatia contributed to the existing knowledge of the stars and planetary movements. She edited the third book of Ptolemy's \"Almagest,\" a comprehensive treatise on astronomy. Her work helped to spread the Alexandrian tradition of astronomy, which was one of the most advanced of the age.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Invention and Innovation</strong><br>\nHypatia was credited with the invention of the astrolabe and the hydroscope. The astrolabe was an instrument used by Greek astronomers to solve problems related to time and the position of the stars. The hydroscope was a device that allowed for the viewing of objects underwater. These inventions showcased her ability to apply mathematical principles to practical uses.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hypatia's Role in Society</strong><br>\nBeyond her scientific work, Hypatia was a symbol of learning and culture in Alexandria. She played an influential role in the political and social life of the city. Hypatia was respected by leaders and thinkers, which was exceptional for a woman in a male-dominated society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
