          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Library of Alexandria</strong><br>\nHypatia was associated with the Library of Alexandria, the center of knowledge in the ancient world. While there's no direct evidence that she worked there, the library symbolized the type of intellectual environment in which she thrived. Her association with this institution underscores her standing as one of the leading intellectuals of her time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Murder of Hypatia</strong><br>\nHypatia's prominence in scholarly and civic affairs led to conflict with the growing Christian community. In March of 415 AD, she was murdered by a mob of Christian zealots, which marked a dramatic and tragic end to her life. Her death is often seen as a symbol of the end of the classical era in Alexandria.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Hypatia</strong><br>\nHypatia's legacy endures as a pioneer for women in the fields of mathematics, philosophy, and science. She is remembered for her intelligence, her teaching, and her tragic death, which has been interpreted by historians as a notable event in the conflict between old and new ideologies. Hypatia remains an inspirational figure in the history of female intellectuals.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
